{"results":[{"url":"https://wset.com/news/nation-world/judge-dismisses-case-against-ex-fbi-officials-nikki-cole-floris-bradley-benavides-accused-of-lying-congress-about-hunter-biden-laptop-probe-justice-department-weaponization-probable-cause-grand-jury-indictment","final_url":"https://wset.com/news/nation-world/judge-dismisses-case-against-ex-fbi-officials-nikki-cole-floris-bradley-benavides-accused-of-lying-congress-about-hunter-biden-laptop-probe-justice-department-weaponization-probable-cause-grand-jury-indictment","title":"Judge dismisses case against ex-FBI officials accused of lying about Hunter Biden probe","description":"The Justice Department could still seek an indictment from a grand jury.","language":"en","text":"# Judge dismisses case against ex-FBI officials accused of lying about Hunter Biden probe\n\n---\n\nby ALEXX ALTMAN-DEVILBISS | The National News Desk\n\nMon, November 2, 2026 at 11:49 AM\n\n4VIEW ALL PHOTOS\n\nFILE - Hunter Biden arrives for a closed-door deposition on Capitol Hill in Washington, Feb. 28, 2024. AP Photo/J. Scott Applewhite, File)\n\nWASHINGTON (TNND) — A federal judge on Monday dismissed a criminal complaint accusing two former FBI officials of lying to Congress about their knowledge of investigations involving Hunter Biden, sharply criticizing the Justice Department’s handling of the case.\n\n“What has transpired here cannot be described as anything other than weaponization,” U.S. Magistrate Judge Zia Faruqui said in a ruling from the bench, according to The Associated Press.\n\nFaruqui said prosecutors had not established probable cause to move forward with the charges against Nikki Cole Floris and Bradley Benavides. The Justice Department could still seek an indictment from a grand jury.\n\nThe judge pointed to the timing of the investigation, which an FBI agent testified began the day after President Donald Trump delivered a speech revisiting his 2020 election loss. The agent said FBI Director Kash Patel ordered the investigation and closely tracked its progress. He also testified he worried the inquiry could be viewed as political.\n\n“I will not bury my head in the sand and pretend that I cannot read a calendar,” Faruqui said, referring to the decision to bring charges shortly before Tuesday’s midterm voting, the Associated Press reported.\n\nFloris and Benavides were charged last month with making false statements to Congress about their involvement in FBI investigations into Hunter Biden. Both worked for the bureau’s now-dissolved Foreign Influence Task Force, which monitored foreign interference campaigns.\n\nAccording to the criminal complaint, Floris told the House Judiciary Committee in 2023 that she had not discussed Hunter Biden while assigned to the task force. Benavides said he learned the FBI had Hunter Biden’s laptop from Wikipedia in 2023. Investigators alleged that records and communications contradicted their testimony. The two have denied wrongdoing, and the allegations had not been tested at trial.\n\nThe case also drew criticism from Faruqui over prosecutors’ decision to use a criminal complaint instead of seeking a grand jury indictment. Last week, the judge struck an amended complaint that prosecutors had filed overnight with an additional charge, saying it had not first been approved by the court.\n\nThe Justice Department’s allegations concerned statements the former officials made in 2023 about their knowledge of the laptop and the task force’s work. The department has argued that the officials’ testimony misled lawmakers investigating the FBI’s handling of information related to Hunter Biden.\n\nThe ruling is a setback for prosecutors, but it does not prevent them from presenting the allegations to a grand jury.\n\n\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_  \n  \n**Editor's note:** *The Associated Press contributed to this article.*","author":"ALEXX ALTMAN-DEVILBISS | The National News Desk","published_date":"2026-11-02T16:49:11.000Z","latency_ms":240.55254700033402,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}